    faqs: [
      { question: "What is plantar fasciitis?", answer: "Plantar fasciitis is an overuse condition affecting the band of tissue connecting the heel bone to the toes, causing pain and stiffness, particularly with the first steps in the morning." },
      { question: "What causes heel pain?", answer: "Heel pain is commonly caused by plantar fasciitis, often linked to overuse, tight calf muscles, unsupportive footwear, or abnormal foot mechanics such as flat feet or high arches." },
      { question: "Is physiotherapy effective for Plantar Fasciitis Treatment Dubai?", answer: "Yes. Physiotherapy is considered the first-line, evidence-based treatment, combining manual therapy, targeted exercises, and modalities like shockwave or ultrasound therapy." },
      { question: "How long does recovery from plantar fasciitis take?", answer: "Recovery varies — mild cases may improve in a few weeks, while moderate to chronic cases can take several months of consistent rehabilitation." },
      { question: "Can plantar fasciitis heal naturally without treatment?", answer: "Some mild cases improve with rest and footwear changes, but ongoing strain often causes symptoms to persist or worsen without proper physiotherapy." },
      { question: "Is surgery necessary for plantar fasciitis?", answer: "No. Most cases respond well to conservative treatment such as physiotherapy, and surgery is only considered when non-surgical options haven't helped after an extended period." },
      { question: "Are orthotics helpful for plantar fasciitis?", answer: "Yes, orthotics and shoe inserts can help redistribute pressure across the foot and support the arch, often used alongside physiotherapy rather than as a standalone fix." },
      { question: "Which exercises work best for plantar fasciitis?", answer: "Plantar fascia stretches, calf stretches, heel raises, and towel stretches are commonly used, ideally guided by a physiotherapist for correct technique." },
      { question: "Can I continue running with plantar fasciitis?", answer: "This depends on your symptom severity. Your physiotherapist will guide you on activity modification and a safe return-to-running plan." },
      { question: "When should I see a physiotherapist for heel pain?", answer: "As soon as you notice persistent morning heel pain or discomfort after standing or exercise — early assessment generally leads to a smoother recovery." },
      { question: "Is shockwave therapy effective for plantar fasciitis?", answer: "Shockwave therapy can be effective for chronic, degenerated fascia tissue that hasn't responded to rest, stretching, or other conservative treatment." },
      { question: "Can plantar fasciitis return after treatment?", answer: "Yes, it can recur if underlying factors like tight calves, poor footwear, or sudden increases in activity aren't addressed, which is why prevention habits matter." },
      { question: "Does being overweight contribute to plantar fasciitis?", answer: "Yes, additional body weight increases load through the foot arch and plantar fascia, which can contribute to the development or persistence of symptoms." },
      { question: "What is the difference between plantar fasciitis and a heel spur?", answer: "Plantar fasciitis is irritation of the fascia itself, while a heel spur is a bony growth that can develop over time; many people with heel spurs have no pain at all." },
      { question: "Does RamaCare Polyclinic offer personalized treatment plans?", answer: "Yes, every patient receives a comprehensive assessment, and treatment plans are tailored to individual needs rather than a generic approach." }
    ],

