    faqs: [
      { question: "What causes shoulder pain?", answer: "Shoulder pain can stem from rotator cuff injuries, frozen shoulder, impingement, tendinitis, bursitis, muscle strain, arthritis, or repetitive overuse — often influenced by posture and lifestyle habits." },
      { question: "Can physiotherapy help shoulder pain?", answer: "Yes. Physiotherapy addresses the root cause of shoulder pain through manual therapy, targeted exercise, and modalities like ultrasound or electrotherapy, rather than just masking symptoms." },
      { question: "How many physiotherapy sessions are needed for shoulder pain?", answer: "This depends on the severity and type of injury. Mild cases may improve within a few sessions, while conditions like frozen shoulder often require a longer, structured program." },
      { question: "Is shoulder physiotherapy treatment painful?", answer: "Most techniques are designed to be tolerable, with intensity adjusted to your comfort level. Some methods, like dry needling, may cause brief mild discomfort." },
      { question: "What is frozen shoulder?", answer: "Frozen shoulder, or adhesive capsulitis, is a condition where the shoulder capsule thickens and tightens, causing progressive stiffness and reduced range of motion." },
      { question: "What is a rotator cuff injury?", answer: "It refers to damage — whether a strain, partial tear, or full tear — in the muscles and tendons that stabilize and move the shoulder joint." },
      { question: "Can shoulder pain radiate down the arm?", answer: "Yes, especially with rotator cuff involvement or nerve irritation near the shoulder, pain can travel toward the upper arm or elbow." },
      { question: "Do I need surgery for shoulder pain?", answer: "Not necessarily. Many shoulder conditions, including partial rotator cuff tears and impingement, respond well to structured physiotherapy without surgical intervention. Your physiotherapist will advise if a surgical referral is appropriate." },
      { question: "Is dry needling effective for shoulder pain?", answer: "Dry needling can be effective for releasing muscular trigger points and reducing referred pain, particularly when combined with therapeutic exercise." },
      { question: "Can shoulder pain come back after treatment?", answer: "It can, particularly if underlying posture or movement habits aren't addressed. That's why our plans include prevention strategies and home exercise programs." },
      { question: "Does insurance cover physiotherapy at RamaCare?", answer: "Coverage varies by provider and policy. Our reception team can help verify your insurance details before your appointment." },
      { question: "How long does shoulder pain recovery take?", answer: "Recovery timelines vary — mild strains may resolve in a few weeks, while conditions like frozen shoulder can take several months of consistent rehabilitation." },
      { question: "What exercises help with shoulder pain?", answer: "Your physiotherapist may recommend rotator cuff strengthening, scapular stabilization, and gentle mobility exercises tailored to your specific diagnosis." },
      { question: "Can sports-related shoulder injuries be treated without surgery?", answer: "Many sports shoulder injuries respond well to a structured rehabilitation program, especially when caught early and managed with sport-specific rehabilitation." },
      { question: "What's the difference between shoulder impingement and frozen shoulder?", answer: "Impingement involves compression of tendons during movement, usually causing pain with specific motions. Frozen shoulder involves generalized stiffness and progressive loss of range of motion across all directions." },
      { question: "Can poor posture cause shoulder pain?", answer: "Yes, prolonged forward-head and rounded-shoulder posture places extra strain on the shoulder joint and surrounding muscles, often contributing to impingement and chronic discomfort." },
      { question: "How do I book an appointment at RamaCare Polyclinic?", answer: "You can call the clinic directly, message us on WhatsApp, or use our online booking option to schedule a shoulder pain treatment Dubai consultation with one of our physiotherapists." }
    ],

