    faqs: [
      { question: "Can arthritis be cured?", answer: "Most chronic forms of arthritis, such as osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis, are managed rather than cured. However, with the right treatment plan, symptoms can be significantly controlled and quality of life greatly improved." },
      { question: "What is the best treatment for arthritis?", answer: "The best treatment depends on the type and severity of arthritis. Options often include physiotherapy, exercise therapy, pain management, lifestyle changes, and in some cases medical or Ayurvedic supportive therapies, tailored to your diagnosis." },
      { question: "Can physiotherapy help arthritis?", answer: "Yes. Physiotherapy is one of the most effective non-surgical approaches for arthritis, helping to reduce pain, improve mobility, and build supportive muscle strength around affected joints." },
      { question: "Which exercises are safe?", answer: "Low-impact exercises like walking, swimming, and guided strengthening are generally well-tolerated, but the safest exercises depend on your specific joints affected and condition severity. Your physiotherapist will guide appropriate options." },
      { question: "Can arthritis affect young adults?", answer: "Yes. While osteoarthritis is more common with age, conditions like rheumatoid arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, and juvenile arthritis can affect younger adults and even children." },
      { question: "What foods should I avoid?", answer: "This varies by arthritis type — for example, gout patients often need to limit high-purine foods. A personalized dietary discussion with your care team is more useful than generic food lists." },
      { question: "Is walking good for arthritis?", answer: "For most people with arthritis, regular, moderate walking supports joint health and mobility. However, the right amount and intensity depends on your specific condition and should be guided by your physiotherapist." },
      { question: "Can arthritis worsen without treatment?", answer: "Yes. Many forms of arthritis progress gradually if left unmanaged, which is why early assessment and a structured treatment plan are important." },
      { question: "Do I need surgery?", answer: "Most arthritis cases are managed successfully with non-surgical approaches like physiotherapy, exercise, and lifestyle changes. Surgery is typically considered only when conservative treatment no longer provides adequate relief." },
      { question: "Is arthritis hereditary?", answer: "Genetics can play a role in certain types of arthritis, particularly rheumatoid arthritis and some other autoimmune conditions, though lifestyle and environmental factors also contribute." },
      { question: "Can Ayurveda help manage arthritis?", answer: "Ayurvedic supportive therapies may be included as part of a broader, personalized treatment plan for some patients, depending on clinical assessment and diagnosis." },
      { question: "How many physiotherapy sessions will I need?", answer: "This varies based on the type and severity of arthritis, as well as individual progress. Some patients benefit from a short structured program, while others require ongoing periodic sessions." },
      { question: "How is rheumatoid arthritis different from osteoarthritis?", answer: "Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune condition causing joint inflammation, often affecting joints symmetrically with pronounced morning stiffness. Osteoarthritis results from cartilage wear over time and tends to worsen with activity." },
      { question: "When should I see a doctor?", answer: "If you have persistent joint pain, ongoing swelling, stiffness that limits daily activities, or symptoms that are getting worse, it's a good idea to have your joints properly assessed." },
      { question: "Why choose RamaCare for arthritis treatment?", answer: "RamaCare Polyclinic combines DHA-licensed medical and physiotherapy expertise with personalized, multidisciplinary treatment plans designed for long-term joint health, not just temporary symptom relief." }
    ],
